body:
> Why not just use the parent node of the template when the template element is popped off the stack?

<img align="right" alt="stan kelly’s haha-yes sicko" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6257356/191922805-aa3c2616-dc53-445d-998d-60c9f232bbc1.png" width="100">

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>

<script>
  customElements.define("innocent-kids", class extends HTMLTemplateElement {
    constructor() {
      sickos.append(super());

      console.warn("innocent-kids moved to sickos @ [create an element for a token](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#create-an-element-for-the-token)");
      console.debug(document.body.getInnerHTML());
    }
  }, { extends: "template" });

  addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
    console.warn("honest-viewers remained top-of-stack and still became the shadowroot host");
    console.debug(document.body.getInnerHTML());
  });
</script>

<div id=sickos></div>

<div id=honest-viewers>
  nothing much to do chief
  <template is=innocent-kids shadowroot=open>haha yes</template>
  we may as well close up shop
</div>
```

![corresponding console output in edge. the first shows the early reparenting, the second shows that it the shadow host did not change in response](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6257356/191919760-a7d53c8c-ac23-47ba-aedf-293552addb03.png)

(Guessing there are ways to reach spots like that w/o customized built-ins, but it was the first thing that came to mind as a hook.)

